--TEST--
`as property<T>` admits only a public declared property of `T`, checking a written-out name where it is written and a computed one where it arrives — `rule:types/property-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Animal {
    public string $name = "rex";
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    public int $legs = 4;
}

// § 2 row 3, and the direction that cannot go wrong: a checked name is still a
// name, so this is total and free.
property<Animal> $written = "name" as property<Animal>;
echo $written as string, "\n";

// § 2 row 1's door. The operand is computed, so the set it must be one of is
// tested when the statement runs rather than where it is written.
string $computed = "na" . "me";
property<Animal> $checked = $computed as property<Animal>;
echo $checked as string, "\n";

// § 2 row 2, a narrowing — and § 3's contravariance in the argument: every
// name an `Animal` key can hold is a name a `Dog` has, so this direction runs
// and the other one does not.
property<Dog> $narrowed = $written as property<Dog>;
echo $narrowed as string, "\n";

// A key travels through a parameter and a return like any other value: it is a
// name, and its representation is the one a `string` already has.
class Pick {
    public static function through(property<Dog> $key): property<Dog> {
        return $key;
    }
}
echo Pick::through($narrowed) as string, "\n";

// `rule:expressions/nullable-conversion-availability` over § 2's rows: the sugar answers `null` exactly where the
// checked form throws.
string $absent = "leg" . "s";
?property<Animal> $missed = $absent as ?property<Animal>;
echo $missed == null ? "null" : "some", "\n";
?property<Dog> $found = $absent as ?property<Dog>;
echo $found == null ? "null" : "some", "\n";

// § 2's row is a row of `rule:types/conversion`'s grid, so a miss throws rather than
// substituting, and the message names the class and the name it was given.
try {
    property<Animal> $bad = $absent as property<Animal>;
    echo "unreachable\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// A name that names a `private` property and a name that names nothing are the
// same failure: visibility is decided at the conversion, once.
class Vault {
    public string $label = "open";
    private string $secret = "shut";
}
string $hidden = "sec" . "ret";
try {
    property<Vault> $peek = $hidden as property<Vault>;
    echo "unreachable\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
name
name
name
name
null
some
`"legs"` is not one of `Animal`'s public declared properties: `$name`
`"secret"` is not one of `Vault`'s public declared properties: `$label`
